d per the rules.\n\n**1. Mismatch: signal shutdown never gives git the SIGTERM grace period.** *(shutdown, job-pool; read)*\n`jobs.ts` says a cancelled group gets SIGTERM so git can remove its lock files, then SIGKILL after `GRACE_MS`. But the CLI signal handler in `src/commands/ary.ts` calls `server.close()` and then `process.exit(code)` in the same tick. `server.close` runs `pool.cancelAll()`, which sends SIGTERM. `process.exit` then fires the `exit` listener, `killAllNow`, which sends SIGKILL to the same groups immediately. So on SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT the grace period is skipped and git gets SIGKILL. The tests pass because the processes do die, but the comment's stated reason for the grace period does not hold on this path. Impact is probably small, since the `ary` git calls look read-only, but it is not what the comments say.\n\n**2. Mismatch: \"pure function\" in the desk-split intent.** *(desk-split; read)*\n`buildDesk` calls `listRefs()`, `gitTry`, `resolveCommits` and `buildPageData`, so it runs git synchronously. Its own comment says \"Runs git synchronously\". The intent should say \"no HTTP; runs in the job's child process\", not \"pure\".\n\n**3. Missing risk: the child's execArgv is not forwarded.** *(job-pool, job-child; read, not verified)*\n`spawn(process.execPath, [JOB, …])` passes no `execArgv`. The bin uses source mode when `process.features.typescript` is set. On Node versions where type stripping needs `--experimental-strip-types` (22.6 to 22.17, by my reading), the parent runs `.ts` source but the child `node page-job.ts` gets no flag, fails, and every page returns 500. On Node 22.18+, 23.6+ and the engines floor of 20.12 (which uses `dist/`), it works. I did not run this.\n\n**4. Missing risk: the memory bound omits the child process.** *(job-pool; read)*\nThe docs say each build holds up to 2 × `GIT_GLOSS_ARY_MAX_OUTPUT`, so 256 MiB for 4 builds. That counts only the parent's buffers. Each child also holds the full page data for up to 200 commits, plus `execFileSync` git output, and nothing caps that. The output cap does not bound it.\n\n**5. Possible slot leak on spawn error.** *(job-pool; read, low confidence)*\n`child.on('error')` settles the job but removes the entry from `this.live` only in `close`. If Node emits `error` without a following `close`, that slot stays taken for the life of the server. I did not check Node's behaviour here, and `process.execPath` makes this unlikely in practice.\n\n**6. Unverified claim in the TODO.** *(docs; read)*\n\"a page costs a node start, about 100 ms\" is not measured anywhere in the diff.\n\n**What checks out (read):**\n- The desk-split move is faithful: the `buildDesk` body matches the old `deskPage`, apart from the result object and the validation now running before git.\n- The 503 path starts nothing and does not count non-page requests, as stated.\n- The 502 path clears its chunks and settles once, and the slot is held until the child closes.\n- `dist/` builds one JS file per source file, so `page-job.js` exists for an installed package.\n- The timeout clamp at 2³¹−1 matches the README.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
