ld be corrected, or the close handler should wait for the group to empty before SIGKILL.\n\n2. **Mismatch (read, admitted in TODO): signal exit skips the grace.** `src/commands/ary.ts:36` calls `server.close()` and then `process.exit()` in the same tick. The exit handler `killAllNow` then SIGKILLs at once. The intent for `shutdown` (\"kill running builds on close, exit and signals\") is met. The grace claim in the `job-pool` comment is not, as in finding 1.\n\n3. **Missing risk (read): silent fallback on bad env values.** `posInt` in `jobs.ts` turns `GIT_GLOSS_ARY_JOBS=0`, `abc` or `-1` into the default with no message. Nothing in the README says so. `GIT_GLOSS_ARY_TIMEOUT` is different: `parseGitTimeout` does the parsing, and I did not check how it handles bad input.\n\n4. **Missing risk (read): detached children are in their own session.** `detached: true` calls `setsid`, so the job is a session leader, not only a group leader. Ctrl-C and a closed terminal therefore do not reach it. The signal handlers cover that, as the README says. But a `kill -9` of the server, or a crash, leaves the builds running. The TODO documents this. The `job-pool` intent (\"own process group\") understates it.\n\n5. **Read, not verified: test-group claims.** \"Hang tests run red on the old synchronous server\" is plausible from the code (the fast-path request waits behind the hung git) but was not run. The `desk-split` claim \"only the return value and the up-front parseQuery differ\" matches a comparison of the old `deskPage` with `buildDesk`. Validation now runs before `listRefs`, and the notes say so.\n\n6. **Checked, no mismatch.** `parseGitTimeout` exists in `src/git.ts:42`. `MAX_RANGE_COMMITS` is re-exported from `server.ts` and used in `desk.ts`. The TODO item that referred to \"the ary synchronous-git item\" was removed, and no other reference to it remains. The README limits (200 commits, 4 jobs, 32 MiB, 256 MiB peak) match the code.\n\n7. **Simplification (read, minor).** The `'error'` handler already frees the slot, and the `'close'` handler then runs again for the same spawn failure. The author notes this as redundant. The `cancelAll()` in `server.close` is also redundant with `closeAllConnections()`, as the author notes. Either can go without changing behaviour.\n\nVERDICT: ISSUES","verdict":"issues"}
